WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.761 position:50% size:92% align:center
A scan combines a row of flags into prefixes. An

00:00:03.761 --> 00:00:06.526 position:50% size:92% align:center
exclusive offset counts selected positions

00:00:06.526 --> 00:00:08.963 position:50% size:92% align:center
strictly before the current position.

00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:12.016 position:50% size:92% align:center
The GPU question is how every thread reads the

00:00:12.016 --> 00:00:14.665 position:50% size:92% align:center
old stage while the block builds a new one.

00:00:16.685 --> 00:00:19.388 position:50% size:92% align:center
The block’s shared values begin as the eight

00:00:19.388 --> 00:00:22.274 position:50% size:92% align:center
flags. At each partner distance, a thread needs

00:00:22.274 --> 00:00:24.301 position:50% size:92% align:center
a value from the preceding stage.

00:00:24.521 --> 00:00:27.471 position:50% size:92% align:center
Shared memory lets threads exchange values, but

00:00:27.471 --> 00:00:30.169 position:50% size:92% align:center
it does not automatically keep those values

00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:32.052 position:50% size:92% align:center
unchanged during the exchange.

00:00:34.072 --> 00:00:37.079 position:50% size:92% align:center
Each participating thread first captures its old

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:40.024 position:50% size:92% align:center
partner value in the private variable previous.

00:00:40.244 --> 00:00:42.674 position:50% size:92% align:center
All threads then reach a barrier before any

00:00:42.674 --> 00:00:44.369 position:50% size:92% align:center
thread is allowed to overwrite

00:00:44.369 --> 00:00:45.556 position:50% size:92% align:center
the old shared stage.

00:00:47.576 --> 00:00:49.998 position:50% size:92% align:center
After that barrier, threads update shared

00:00:49.998 --> 00:00:52.715 position:50% size:92% align:center
storage. Another block barrier completes those

00:00:52.715 --> 00:00:55.491 position:50% size:92% align:center
writes before the next partner distance begins.

00:00:55.711 --> 00:00:58.309 position:50% size:92% align:center
The barriers protect two different boundaries:

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:00.343 position:50% size:92% align:center
old reads before overwrites, and new

00:01:00.343 --> 00:01:01.812 position:50% size:92% align:center
writes before later reads.

00:01:03.832 --> 00:01:06.807 position:50% size:92% align:center
Imagine thread one has already replaced its old

00:01:06.807 --> 00:01:09.528 position:50% size:92% align:center
flag with one, while thread two still needs

00:01:09.528 --> 00:01:11.427 position:50% size:92% align:center
thread one’s old flag of zero.

00:01:11.647 --> 00:01:14.629 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict whether a barrier only after

00:01:14.629 --> 00:01:17.741 position:50% size:92% align:center
the writes would protect that old value. This is

00:01:17.741 --> 00:01:19.881 position:50% size:92% align:center
a possible race illustration, not

00:01:19.881 --> 00:01:21.567 position:50% size:92% align:center
an executed unsafe kernel.

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:30.251 position:50% size:92% align:center
A later barrier cannot restore a value that was

00:01:30.251 --> 00:01:33.053 position:50% size:92% align:center
already overwritten. Capturing partners first,

00:01:33.053 --> 00:01:35.369 position:50% size:92% align:center
then collectively ordering the writes,

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:37.136 position:50% size:92% align:center
preserves the intended stage.

00:01:37.356 --> 00:01:39.965 position:50% size:92% align:center
The private working value and the block’s shared

00:01:39.965 --> 00:01:42.519 position:50% size:92% align:center
array solve different parts of that dependency.
